{"t":1788912848,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"32fb3176-4e56-4e6a-8bbb-183ae69e3def","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-08T18:39:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 081839\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n239 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nAdded a slight chance of showers Wednesday over portions of the\nShenandoah Valley and eastern West Virginia panhandle.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Mostly dry through mid week.\n\n- 2) Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\n- 3) Cooler and less humid conditions are expected on Friday,\n  before humidity builds back in this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry through mid week.\n\nConditions will remain tranquil as high pressure persists over \nthe Mid-Atlantic through Wednesday before gradually moving \noffshore. Large scale subsidence and dry air lead to a mostly\nprecipitation-free forecast with temperatures rising to several \ndegrees above normal as the high pulls offshore.\n\nA weak surface trough in the wake of gradually departing high\npressure may be enough to produce a few showers mainly over the\nShenandoah Valley/I-81 corridor into the eastern WV panhandle\nfrom late Wednesday morning through late Wednesday afternoon.\n\nHigh temperatures may approach 90 Wednesday (slightly cooler in\nthe mountains). Low temperatures (and dew points) will be in \nthe 50s and 60s during this time. Some river valley fog is \nlikely each morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm chances return Thursday.\n\nThe upper-level ridge will begin to flatten out Wednesday before \nbreaking down and pushing south and east into Thursday. Several \npieces of upper-level energy rotating around an upper low \ncentered over Hudson Bay will slowly push a cold front south \ntowards our region by Thursday. At the surface, high pressure \npushing offshore will bring increasing warmth and moisture \nto the area, with highs in the 80s to near 90 especially south\nof US-50/I-66 with dew points in the upper 60s to low 70s. Mid-\nlevel moisture will be increasing on westerly flow, but that\nintroduces a downsloping component in the lower levels. This\nreduces the amount of CAPE/DCAPE, especially when given\nanticipated cloud cover over the northern half of the area, for\nany potential severe weather. Still, a few clusters particularly\nfrom central VA to southern MD could contain gusty winds and\nheavy downpours Thursday, mainly in the afternoon and evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Cooler and less humid conditions are expected \non Friday, before humidity builds back in this weekend. \n\nHigh pressure will build to our north on Friday in the wake of \nThursday's frontal passage. Cooler and drier air will advect into \nthe region from the north in advance of the approaching high.\nPartly to mostly sunny skies are expected, with high temperatures\nin the upper 70s and lower 80s. It will also feel considerably \nless humid, with dewpoints dropping into the upper 50s and lower\n60s. Some shower or thunderstorm activity is possible near and\nsouth of I-64 as the front stalls just off to the south.\n\nHigh pressure will move offshore and a strong area of low pressure \nwill track across Hudson Bay on Saturday, causing winds to turn out \nof the south in response. Southerly winds will advect a more humid \nairmass back into the area, with dewpoints climbing into the upper \n60s and lower 70s. Chances for showers and thunderstorms will \naccompany the more humid airmass. Both instability and shear values \nare forecast to remain relatively modest, so any storms that do\nform likely stay on the weaker side.\n\nThere is still some model divergence for Sunday. What we do know is \nthat the humidity will stick around, bringing chances for showers \nand thunderstorms. However, there is still some uncertainty as to \nwhether or not the marine layer will mix out. If not, temperatures \nwill be cooler with plenty of clouds. If so, there will be some \nbreaks of sunshine and it will be warmer. Guidance leans slightly \ntoward the second scenario, but this will have to be monitored as it \ngets closer.\n\nHigh pressure may return behind a weak cold front for Monday and \nTuesday, bringing drier conditions, but temperatures will most \nlikely still be above climo.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail through the end of the\nweek, with two exceptions. Early morning river valley fog could\naffect mainly KCHO/KMRB each day roughly 08z-13z (confidence too\nlow to include in TAF as of 18Z). Then, some shower and t-storm\nactivity could bring brief restrictions on Thursday (mainly in \nthe afternoon and/or evening). Winds remain S/SW through \nThursday until a cold front brings a shift to W/NW.\n\nLow clouds are possible late Friday night into Saturday with\nsub-VFR conditions. Showers are possible Saturday along with a \npossible afternoon thunderstorm.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nWinds may increase in modest channeling tonight over the lower\ntidal Potomac River and the main channel of the Chesapeake Bay,\nbut are more likely to increase Wednesday afternoon into \nWednesday night. Winds shift to the west and northwest and could\nremain gusty Thursday as a cold front crosses, possibly \nbringing a few showers and thunderstorms.\n\nSub-SCA level north to northwesterly winds are most likely over the \nwaters on Friday. Winds will shift to the south on Saturday, and \ncould near low-end SCA levels within channeled southerly flow \nSaturday afternoon into Saturday evening.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTide levels remain around 1 to 1.25 feet above normal. Light\nflow may allow anomalies to drop slightly to around 1 foot \nabove astronomical norms through the middle of the week. \nAnnapolis could reach Minor Stage one or two more times through \nThursday prior to a cold front shifting winds to the west. Other\nsites could approach minor flood at times, as well, mainly late\nWednesday night into early Thursday morning, though west-of-\nsouth flow by this time lowers confidence.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 1 PM Wednesday to 7 AM EDT Thursday \n     for ANZ530>533-538>542.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 4 PM Wednesday to 10 AM EDT Thursday \n     for ANZ534-537-543.\n     Small Craft Advisory from 4 PM Wednesday to 4 AM EDT Thursday \n     for ANZ535-536.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/KLW/CJL/KJP\nAVIATION...KLW/CJL/KJP\nMARINE...KLW/CJL/KJP\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788912817,"days":{"2026-09-08":{"hi":null,"lo":68,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Cloudy"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":90,"lo":72,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-10":{"hi":90,"lo":69,"pop":74,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-09-11":{"hi":84,"lo":65,"pop":17,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":83,"lo":69,"pop":32,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-13":{"hi":87,"lo":69,"pop":21,"short":"Partly Sunny then Slight Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-14":{"hi":87,"lo":68,"pop":17,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-15":{"hi":86,"lo":null,"pop":18,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788912000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,21100,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,4200,4200,4200,6000,6000,6000],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,4.0,4.0,4.0],"spd":[5,4,4,4,4,3,4,4,4,5,5,5,6,7,8,8,9,10